        "subject": "LU-20730 llapi: read and write LFU Object Stream chunks",
        "message": "LU-20730 llapi: read and write LFU Object Stream chunks\n\nA scan passes its caller one object per callback, as a struct that\nholds pointers and file descriptors. Such a record cannot be saved to\na file or sent to another process, and a caller that wants many\nobjects at once has to collect them itself.\n\nThe LFU Object Stream is a format for many objects at once: a buffer\nof fixed-size slots, each holding one chunk, which is a Cap'n Proto\nLfuChunk message with a list of LfuInode records and their EA values.\nTimothy Day defined it in lfu.capnp.\n\nAdd lfu.capnp to include/uapi/linux/lustre, installed with the other\nheaders, and lustre_lfu_capnp.h with its enum values and the offsets\nof the fields, so that C code needs no Cap'n Proto compiler. LfuInode\ncarries the object's inode number on the target and the nanoseconds\nof its four timestamps, besides the seconds, and LfuEa carries the\nencryption context, which says whether a directory's names are\nencrypted.\n\nAdd liblustreapi_lfu.c, which writes and reads the chunks that the\nLustre scanners produce. The writer builds a chunk in place, records\nfrom the front of the slot and EA values from the back, so nothing is\ncopied twice. It stays internal (lustreapi_lfu.h) until a scanner uses\nit. The reader is public: llapi_scan_chunk_parse(),\nllapi_scan_chunk_inode() and llapi_scan_chunk_skipped(). It checks\nevery pointer against the slot and refuses what it cannot follow, and\na record written by an older schema reads as zero past its end.\nstruct llapi_scan_inode keeps spare room, so a field added to the\nschema later does not change its size.\n\nllapi_lfu_test, run by sanity 56En, reads a chunk that libcapnp wrote\nand one whose LfuInode records are 12 data words, as an older writer\nwould produce, writes and reads back records over 16 slots, and checks\na record too large for its slot, a chunk of skipped objects only, bad\ninput, and every one-bit corruption of a slot.\n\nTest-Parameters: trivial\nSigned-off-by: Artem Blagodarenko <ablagodarenko@thelustrecollective.com>\nSigned-off-by: Timothy Day <timday@thelustrecollective.com>\nAssisted-by: ClaudeCode:claude-opus-5-5\nChange-Id: I2c5c9177f4446c10ddd962d1bb35f5c2bc846fe1\n"
      },
      "branch": "refs/heads/master"
    }
